{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:27:30.759217Z","id":"ddb5fa1ff58e7b5796f55a206ab8722ed294a1dc160885c63a4a2a58457ab8a1","body":{"detail":"open() inserts into REGISTRY.stores and the VFS inserts into REGISTRY.files, and nothing removes them except a later open() of the same name. When workerd evicts one Durable Object but keeps its isolate for others, the entry (a JS SqlStorage reference and an empty dirty set) stays until the isolate dies. A bounded leak per object, harmless for a proof, worth a drop hook (remove on the last xClose of the main file) before many objects share an isolate. The registry is also per thread: a Connection moved to another thread gets SQLITE_IOERR, which is fine on wasm32 but matters if the VFS is used natively.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":292},"end":{"line":302},"content_hash":"90e6535beb6055a4f599536aed2839a54b46e2ace5ec7becca3aed81484d414c"},"summary":"The thread-local registry never forgets a database file: an evicted Durable Object's store handle and file entry stay in an isolate that lives on","tags":["vfs","memory","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:27:30.760030Z","id":"322eb50e48e36eba94ec275abe2fd48279b1630774e1398afeb2af881267255a","body":{"detail":"StoredFile::chunk reads the store for any chunk not in the dirty set. With SQLite's default cache_size (about 2 MB) a scan of a larger database rereads every chunk: GET /do/demo/stats?check=1 on a 10 MiB database made about 2069 store reads per run. Raising PRAGMA cache_size (memory allows it: 128 MB per isolate) is the cheap fix; a clean cache in the VFS would duplicate SQLite's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":125},"end":{"line":146},"content_hash":"b94a4793dd2ae35771bedc23a50a323e03c2fc30b4434c5d757dc9d7b06871c1"},"summary":"Reads past SQLite's page cache go to ctx.storage.sql every time: there is no clean-chunk cache","tags":["vfs","performance","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:46:35.580135Z","id":"49d6c9d08c7c7ec61eed813e1287c75ea88541b84770942570d34496adc89695","body":{"detail":"Failure: any caller that runs PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL on a connection from open() (crates/clax-core/src/store/exec.rs:70 does, for its writer). Under locking_mode=EXCLUSIVE SQLite accepts WAL without shared memory; the -wal file is not a registered store name, so add_file (line 316) makes it a MemChunksFile and every COMMIT lands in memory. Only the header switch to WAL reaches the store, so after an eviction the next open() fails with SQLITE_CANTOPEN (\"unable to open database file\") and the committed rows are gone. Shown by the ignored test wal_mode_commits_reach_the_store in crates/clax-worker/vfs/tests/adversarial.rs:75:94 (cargo test -p clax-do-vfs --test adversarial -- --include-ignored). The same applies to a later PRAGMA locking_mode=NORMAL or journal_mode=OFF (OFF breaks ROLLBACK after a spill).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":488},"end":{"line":522},"content_hash":"d74d4a6e8deb66b729f8a2ead03d93add9134acc172e0939c0835f527947f13f"},"suggested_fix":"Refuse to leave the settings open() relies on: answer SQLITE_FCNTL_PRAGMA in xFileControl for journal_mode (anything but memory) and locking_mode (anything but exclusive) with SQLITE_ERROR, and refuse SQLITE_OPEN_WAL in add_file with SQLITE_CANTOPEN. Port clax-core's store without its WAL switch.","summary":"journal_mode=WAL loses every commit and leaves the database unopenable: nothing pins the journal mode open() sets","tags":["vfs","durability","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:46:35.580470Z","id":"82e2ad25f6ab69d5faf2faf9396920860d26d367975e479015a50496810abc5a","body":{"detail":"Failure: open(\"x.db\", s1) then open(\"x.db\", s2) while the first connection is alive (a Durable Object rebuilt in the same isolate after a reset, while code of the previous instance still runs or its Connection is dropped mid-transaction). with_file/with_file_mut resolve the handle by file_name only, so the old connection's xWrite/xSync reach the new StoredFile and the new store; with locking_mode=EXCLUSIVE its page cache is stale, so its next write discards the new instance's commits. The ignored test a_stale_connection_cannot_write_into_a_later_open (crates/clax-worker/vfs/tests/adversarial.rs:230:262) ends with the new store holding keys [1, 3]: key 2, committed through the new connection, is lost and integrity_check still says ok. The platform's guard (an old instance's ctx.storage stops working) is bypassed because the old connection uses the new store. A second plain connection on the same name (clax-core's reader pool) shares the writer's dirty chunks with no-op locks. Related: ddb5fa1f (the registry never forgets a file).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":363},"end":{"line":383},"content_hash":"f56b7daf019654a00d167182beb630bf10860707963a2daf4788d42d39ed7bbb"},"suggested_fix":"Give each open() a unique SQLite file name (for example \"{generation}/{name}\") mapped to (store, stored file name), drop the entry on the main file's xClose, and refuse a second xOpen of a main file that is already open.","summary":"Files are looked up by name, so a connection from an earlier open() of the same name writes into the later open's store and overwrites its commits","tags":["vfs","durability","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:46:35.580922Z","id":"f55a9ef9e6de41af4a3b447cd6be925bd1b417d878074e40aeaddd90bfe0c1a7","body":{"detail":"A commit that shrinks the file (VACUUM, auto_vacuum) reaches the store in two storage transactions: pages and size at xSync, then the truncation at SQLITE_FCNTL_COMMIT_PHASETWO. When only the second fails, the statement returns SQLITE_IOERR although the first already stored the new database. Test a_refused_truncation_fails_a_vacuum_the_store_already_holds (crates/clax-worker/vfs/tests/adversarial.rs:192:228) shows it: VACUUM errs, a reopen sees the vacuumed page count with the old stored size. The stored state is valid (only unused chunks past the header's page count remain); the problem is a client told a durable commit failed, which a retry of a non-idempotent write would duplicate.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":394},"end":{"line":416},"content_hash":"bef2c85356fee0e347d51bfefa124c47ae5314c8501871c7fe29098c71d14400"},"suggested_fix":"When the PHASETWO flush has no dirty chunks and only a pending truncation, keep truncated_to on failure and return SQLITE_OK; the next commit applies it.","summary":"A refused truncation at COMMIT_PHASETWO reports an I/O error for a transaction the store already holds","tags":["vfs","error-handling","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:46:35.580966Z","id":"03147a92be16d4d804bba2cf203837e4e837f9905fac982ec382834b61f1ee2f","body":{"detail":"pager_playback calls sqlite3PagerSync after replaying the journal, so a ROLLBACK of a transaction whose pages spilled ends in an xSync and a store commit of the restored state. Test a_spilled_rollback_stores_only_the_restored_state (crates/clax-worker/vfs/tests/adversarial.rs:125:154) asserts that one store commit and that its bytes equal the pre-transaction state. The existing test a_rolled_back_transaction_that_spilled_pages_leaves_the_store_unchanged (crates/clax-worker/vfs/tests/vfs.rs:90:109) compares bytes only, so it would also pass if spilled pages were stored mid-transaction and restored by the rollback; a_spilling_transaction_stores_nothing_until_commit (adversarial.rs:96:118) checks that no commit happens before COMMIT.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":8},"end":{"line":17},"content_hash":"f3665022e9d7b6db50a8006176a164341db5d066bd0c687d0b1de275a7f7b831"},"suggested_fix":"Say that a rollback after a spill stores the restored state (equal to the last commit), and keep the commit-count test.","summary":"The module doc says only committed transactions reach the store, but rolling back a spilled transaction also stores (the restored pages)","tags":["vfs","docs","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:46:35.581419Z","id":"741e86707fa0439439ae6d8ec0669d853c4ccfa8a00e45281489ecbdc39e3c3b","body":{"detail":"About 2 MB (-2000) is the native libsqlite3-sys default the tests run with. A 10 MiB database scanned once per isolate fits the wasm cache, so 2069 store reads on GET /do/demo/stats?check=1 points to a cold cache (a freshly constructed object) rather than eviction within a scan; worth re-measuring on a second request to the same instance before raising cache_size.","kind":"comment","references":"322eb50e48e36eba94ec275abe2fd48279b1630774e1398afeb2af881267255a","summary":"On wasm32 the default cache is 16 MiB, not about 2 MB: sqlite-wasm-rs 0.5.5 builds SQLite with SQLITE_DEFAULT_CACHE_SIZE=-16384","tags":["vfs","performance","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.761291Z","id":"b5536071084323b58b1e8dd498f4e54e55b09df8c7c81b46b303c80fbb61afd8","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0bf47593","summary":"Fixed in 0bf47593: xFileControl answers SQLITE_FCNTL_PRAGMA, refusing journal_mode other than MEMORY and locking_mode other than EXCLUSIVE with SQLITE_ERROR and a message; add_file refuses SQLITE_OPEN_WAL, and main-database or journal names open() did not register, with SQLITE_CANTOPEN. Evidence (cargo test --workspace in crates/clax-worker: adversarial 13 passed, 0 ignored; vfs 5 passed): wal_mode_is_refused_so_commits_reach_the_store (the formerly ignored wal_mode_commits_reach_the_store), journal_and_locking_modes_other_than_open_s_are_refused, a_stored_wal_database_is_refused and only_the_files_open_registered_can_be_opened pass; node crates/clax-worker/test-wasm.mjs prints 'ok   journal_mode=WAL is refused' under wrangler dev --local.","supersedes":"49d6c9d08c7c7ec61eed813e1287c75ea88541b84770942570d34496adc89695","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.764029Z","id":"36728ef28ca892c8a97c7209d68d191e38ec2d20dcacceecd2f1b4e020452ff0","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0bf47593","summary":"Fixed in 0bf47593: each open() gives SQLite a file name of its own ('{n}/{name}') mapped to the store and the stored name; a later open() of the name removes the earlier entry, so the earlier connection's reads and writes fail with SQLITE_IOERR; dropping the returned Database removes its entry; a second xOpen of an open database file fails with SQLITE_CANTOPEN. Evidence (cargo test --workspace in crates/clax-worker: adversarial 13 passed, 0 ignored; vfs 5 passed): a_stale_connection_cannot_write_into_a_later_open, no longer ignored, passes (the old connection's INSERT errs, the old store receives no commit, the new store holds keys [1, 2]), as does only_the_files_open_registered_can_be_opened.","supersedes":"82e2ad25f6ab69d5faf2faf9396920860d26d367975e479015a50496810abc5a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.766917Z","id":"fb09f46d891936d14476a4a4a7e77f70b2b78a8bc82bd7f8ace1e41131a54d38","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0bf47593","summary":"Fixed in 0bf47593: StoredFile::commit_phase_two keeps a refused truncation-only flush pending and returns SQLITE_OK; the next commit stores the truncation. Evidence (cargo test --workspace in crates/clax-worker: adversarial 13 passed, 0 ignored; vfs 5 passed): a_refused_truncation_is_kept_for_the_next_commit (VACUUM succeeds with its PHASETWO store commit refused; after the next INSERT the store holds size page_count*4096 and page_count chunks; a reopen passes integrity_check) and a_truncation_never_stored_leaves_a_valid_database pass.","supersedes":"f55a9ef9e6de41af4a3b447cd6be925bd1b417d878074e40aeaddd90bfe0c1a7","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.772933Z","id":"f2239d1da53e93a0640b8039f92b1c2354b7810643947fcd56fa3ba3dd9fe9e1","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0bf47593","summary":"Fixed in 0bf47593: the module doc (crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs:8:20) says SQLite syncs the file when it rolls back a transaction whose pages spilled, so the store receives the restored pages, equal to the last commit. a_rolled_back_transaction_that_spilled_pages_leaves_the_store_unchanged (crates/clax-worker/vfs/tests/vfs.rs) now asserts no store commit while the transaction is open and exactly one after the rollback; it passes (cargo test --workspace in crates/clax-worker: adversarial 13 passed, 0 ignored; vfs 5 passed).","supersedes":"03147a92be16d4d804bba2cf203837e4e837f9905fac982ec382834b61f1ee2f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.775712Z","id":"0954d784847652d7f3940cc39522d9291333b5a1f82eaf6b2deaea2ba69f2da4","body":{"detail":"StoredFile::chunk reads the store for any chunk not in the dirty set. open() sets the page cache to 8 MiB (Limits::cache_bytes, CACHE_BYTES in this file). Before git:0bf47593 the wasm build used sqlite-wasm-rs 0.5.5's default, 16 MiB (SQLITE_DEFAULT_CACHE_SIZE=-16384); about 2 MB (-2000) is the native libsqlite3-sys default. GET /do/demo/stats?check=1 on a 10 MiB database made about 2069 store reads per run under that 16 MiB default: the database fits the cache, so the reads point to a cold cache (a freshly constructed object), not eviction within a scan. With 8 MiB a 10 MiB scan no longer fits. Re-measure on a second request to the same instance before raising cache_size (memory: see Limits::default) or adding a clean cache in the VFS, which would duplicate SQLite's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":201},"end":{"line":221},"content_hash":"06506f0ebf5ef6c73259738544bd7626734ae04007c77230e802592e9d6ea9f4"},"summary":"Reads past SQLite's page cache go to ctx.storage.sql every time: there is no clean-chunk cache","supersedes":"322eb50e48e36eba94ec275abe2fd48279b1630774e1398afeb2af881267255a","tags":["vfs","performance","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.777054Z","id":"dee5e5feb2f39fb18a71000352150ad69595300689c2a3c0b3c5e72caed2ed5b","body":{"detail":"open() registers each file under a SQLite name of its own; OpenFile::drop (crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs:719:731), run when the Database is dropped, removes it, and a later open() of the same stored name removes the earlier entry. Whether workerd drops an evicted Durable Object's Rust struct (wasm-bindgen frees it when its JS wrapper is freed or collected) was not checked, so an object evicted while its isolate lives on may keep its JS SqlStorage reference and file entry in REGISTRY until the isolate dies: a bounded leak per object. The registry is per thread: a Database used from another thread gets SQLITE_IOERR, fine on wasm32, relevant if the VFS is used natively.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":405},"end":{"line":416},"content_hash":"ad255d92f317fc9cfe8b0b745062940f6a9279360f152b368334c6c05f5ac9c4"},"summary":"The registry forgets a database file only when its Database is dropped or a later open() of the name replaces it: an evicted Durable Object whose Rust object is never dropped keeps its entry","supersedes":"ddb5fa1ff58e7b5796f55a206ab8722ed294a1dc160885c63a4a2a58457ab8a1","tags":["vfs","memory","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.783990Z","id":"d32c54dfd358b8e3462d794880da39ba4be538a7f56caaf492582bff1cabca2c","body":{"detail":"Overriding SQLiteIoMethods::xClose would forget the file whenever SQLite closes it, whoever holds the connection. It is an unsafe extern \"C\" fn, a third function allowed unsafe in a crate whose unsafe exception waits on the owner (f11bf9fe), so open() returns a Database (a Connection behind Deref) whose drop forgets the file instead.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":719},"end":{"line":731},"content_hash":"f75eb7681dfa13f58930c4e87025aecaae596bf66fb502328969365d7b56d102"},"summary":"Forget a database file in the main file's xClose, rather than in Database's drop","tags":["revisit:f11bf9fe allows unsafe in further VFS callbacks","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T04:24:31.784039Z","id":"0b1e9b6842cf7598ad0bc20141ba0b92784e49cdbdb569c272df225b134755e0","body":{"detail":"The 8 MiB cache and 16 MiB cap are chosen against a 128 MB isolate from how the dirty set, the memory journal and the page cache grow; nothing measured wasm memory. Reading WebAssembly.Memory's buffer size after a 15 MiB commit and after a refused 17 MiB one (POST /do/{name}/fill?mb=15 and ?mb=17 under wrangler dev --local) would confirm them. The cap also fails a VACUUM of a database over 16 MiB and any migration that rewrites more than 16 MiB in one transaction, so a port of clax-core's store must batch such rewrites.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":118},"end":{"line":140},"content_hash":"3634198fbf75ad1d2d8c5738c38e90d3a452878299059bb7a29791057789d110"},"summary":"Limits::default's memory figures (40 MiB for a commit, about 56 MiB for one refused at the cap) are worked out from SQLite's journal behaviour, not measured in workerd","tags":["vfs","memory","limits","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T06:18:39.903447Z","id":"433bd573dce39a3cbf6753ae0e5c33ba94af9f222542ab56137f1b144e14d0ee","body":{"detail":"Overriding SQLiteIoMethods::xClose would forget the file whenever SQLite closes it, whoever holds the connection. It is an unsafe extern \"C\" fn, a third function allowed unsafe in a crate whose unsafe exception waits on the owner (f11bf9fe), so open() returns a Database (a Connection behind Deref) whose drop forgets the file instead.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":730},"end":{"line":742},"content_hash":"f75eb7681dfa13f58930c4e87025aecaae596bf66fb502328969365d7b56d102"},"summary":"Forget a database file in the main file's xClose, rather than in Database's drop","supersedes":"d32c54dfd358b8e3462d794880da39ba4be538a7f56caaf492582bff1cabca2c","tags":["revisit:f11bf9fe allows unsafe in further VFS callbacks","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:15:09.057836Z","id":"a5c610bed5d72e94765e5ca59e2603e9d288e454e19a7146ea7bae718103904f","body":{"detail":"The 8 MiB cache and 16 MiB cap are chosen against a 128 MB isolate from how the dirty set, the memory journal and the page cache grow; nothing measured wasm memory. Reading WebAssembly.Memory's buffer size after a 15 MiB commit and after a refused 17 MiB one (POST /do/{name}/fill?mb=15 and ?mb=17 under wrangler dev --local) would confirm them. The cap also fails a VACUUM of a database over 16 MiB and any migration that rewrites more than 16 MiB in one transaction, so a port of clax-core's store must batch such rewrites.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":131},"end":{"line":153},"content_hash":"3634198fbf75ad1d2d8c5738c38e90d3a452878299059bb7a29791057789d110"},"summary":"Limits::default's memory figures (40 MiB for a commit, about 56 MiB for one refused at the cap) are worked out from SQLite's journal behaviour, not measured in workerd","supersedes":"0b1e9b6842cf7598ad0bc20141ba0b92784e49cdbdb569c272df225b134755e0","tags":["vfs","memory","limits","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:15:09.062620Z","id":"6d776ac508026352690da481154c2a595da64b5c8cedfed151a0929b008646b4","body":{"detail":"StoredFile::chunk reads the store for any chunk not in the dirty set. open() sets the page cache to 8 MiB (Limits::cache_bytes, CACHE_BYTES in this file). Before git:0bf47593 the wasm build used sqlite-wasm-rs 0.5.5's default, 16 MiB (SQLITE_DEFAULT_CACHE_SIZE=-16384); about 2 MB (-2000) is the native libsqlite3-sys default. GET /do/demo/stats?check=1 on a 10 MiB database made about 2069 store reads per run under that 16 MiB default: the database fits the cache, so the reads point to a cold cache (a freshly constructed object), not eviction within a scan. With 8 MiB a 10 MiB scan no longer fits. Re-measure on a second request to the same instance before raising cache_size (memory: see Limits::default) or adding a clean cache in the VFS, which would duplicate SQLite's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":214},"end":{"line":234},"content_hash":"06506f0ebf5ef6c73259738544bd7626734ae04007c77230e802592e9d6ea9f4"},"summary":"Reads past SQLite's page cache go to ctx.storage.sql every time: there is no clean-chunk cache","supersedes":"0954d784847652d7f3940cc39522d9291333b5a1f82eaf6b2deaea2ba69f2da4","tags":["vfs","performance","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:15:09.067021Z","id":"2a648ff27eb4938d374df80edcc6b6db96715f98b274c6d5f7e957c375aff055","body":{"detail":"open() registers each file under a SQLite name of its own; OpenFile::drop (crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs:750:763), run when the Database is dropped, removes it and its Registry::handles entries, and a later open() of the same stored name removes the earlier file entry. Whether workerd drops an evicted Durable Object's Rust struct (wasm-bindgen frees it when its JS wrapper is freed or collected) was not checked, so an object evicted while its isolate lives on may keep its JS SqlStorage reference and file entry in REGISTRY until the isolate dies: a bounded leak per object. Temporary files without SQLITE_OPEN_DELETEONCLOSE also keep their handles entry until another name is recorded at the same address. The registry is per thread: a Database used from another thread gets SQLITE_IOERR, fine on wasm32, relevant if the VFS is used natively.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":424},"end":{"line":448},"content_hash":"d9765fc2421c68d00daebfa61c6d37b70e72c1446380b0fcf9b6ea138b72a0b5"},"summary":"The registry forgets a database file only when its Database is dropped or a later open() of the name replaces it: an evicted Durable Object whose Rust object is never dropped keeps its entry","supersedes":"dee5e5feb2f39fb18a71000352150ad69595300689c2a3c0b3c5e72caed2ed5b","tags":["vfs","memory","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:15:09.076238Z","id":"90ff39ed3f5d29e5c7403688c5a66d18cc22d41663af77ad1ff928823c5d4746","body":{"detail":"Overriding SQLiteIoMethods::xClose would forget the file whenever SQLite closes it, whoever holds the connection. It is an unsafe extern \"C\" fn, and the crate forbids unsafe code (#![forbid(unsafe_code)] in crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs; the owner's decision on f11bf9fe), so open() returns a Database (a Connection behind Deref) whose drop forgets the file instead.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":750},"end":{"line":763},"content_hash":"f5db76665c944e9249b0dbc1f3c85baf953474ce0f462d7ccaec87f9afca569b"},"summary":"Forget a database file in the main file's xClose, rather than in Database's drop","supersedes":"433bd573dce39a3cbf6753ae0e5c33ba94af9f222542ab56137f1b144e14d0ee","tags":["revisit:rsqlite-vfs offers a safe close hook on VfsStore or VfsFile","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:15:09.084153Z","id":"6a815f038e3e970747cc901306ec3795a01e05c9873822bdab97a9efe6d297fc","body":{"detail":"open_with installs authorize() with Connection::authorizer (crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs:841). Database derefs to its Connection and into_parts hands the Connection out, so a caller that calls authorizer() itself replaces the refusals: journal_mode=OFF or synchronous=OFF would then go through, and a commit under synchronous=OFF never reaches the store (no xSync). journal_mode=WAL and the file-backed journal modes still fail at the VFS (add_file refuses a WAL file and any journal file). The refusal error is SQLITE_AUTH with SQLite's message \"not authorized\"; it does not say which setting the VFS needs. Nothing in crates/clax-core or crates/clax-worker installs an authorizer today.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":589},"end":{"line":626},"content_hash":"aa49a3fc5f87041d9185591fd6539b7935a782ecec6991236a6377daad45db75"},"suggested_fix":"If a caller needs its own authorizer, export authorize() and have the caller's authorizer delegate to it for AuthAction::Pragma.","summary":"The journal_mode, locking_mode and synchronous refusals live in the connection's authorizer, which a caller can replace, and refuse with a bare \"not authorized\"","tags":["vfs","durability","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:39:24.035692Z","id":"da73c815d9bf083084b7b77017482956df536d901860e6559cf816841a37ebd3","body":{"detail":"Overriding SQLiteIoMethods::xClose would forget the file whenever SQLite closes it, whoever holds the connection. It is an unsafe extern \"C\" fn, and the crate forbids unsafe code (#![forbid(unsafe_code)] in crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs; the owner's decision on f11bf9fe), so open() returns a Database (a Connection behind Deref) whose drop forgets the file instead.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":756},"end":{"line":769},"content_hash":"f5db76665c944e9249b0dbc1f3c85baf953474ce0f462d7ccaec87f9afca569b"},"summary":"Forget a database file in the main file's xClose, rather than in Database's drop","supersedes":"90ff39ed3f5d29e5c7403688c5a66d18cc22d41663af77ad1ff928823c5d4746","tags":["revisit:rsqlite-vfs offers a safe close hook on VfsStore or VfsFile","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-worker/vfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:39:24.045419Z","id":"f37f39c67d876eba4b326af9e403eaf4df8b60f10a99375b4604b49d6ea38657","body":{"detail":"open_with installs authorize() with Connection::authorizer. Database derefs to its Connection and into_parts hands the Connection out, so a caller that calls authorizer() itself replaces the refusals unless its authorizer delegates to the exported authorize() (as a_caller_authorizer_that_delegates_keeps_the_refusals in crates/clax-worker/vfs/tests/adversarial.rs does): journal_mode=OFF or synchronous=OFF would then go through, and a commit under synchronous=OFF never reaches the store (no xSync). journal_mode=WAL and the file-backed journal modes still fail at the VFS (add_file refuses a WAL file and any journal file). The refusal error is SQLITE_AUTH with SQLite's message \"not authorized\"; it does not say which setting the VFS needs. Nothing in crates/clax-core or crates/clax-worker installs an authorizer today.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":589},"end":{"line":631},"content_hash":"992568dacb224119ce17bf93fe1d5cfc95e3f698d5af0bd48af5ad6cd3709adc"},"suggested_fix":"A caller that needs its own authorizer answers authorize(ctx) first whenever it is not Allow.","summary":"The journal_mode, locking_mode and synchronous refusals live in the connection's authorizer, which a caller can replace, and refuse with a bare \"not authorized\"","supersedes":"6a815f038e3e970747cc901306ec3795a01e05c9873822bdab97a9efe6d297fc","tags":["vfs","durability","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
